    "tier": "consensus_core",
    "priority": "strong",
    "case_for": "Monk's second Columbia album captures his legendary working quartet (Rouse, Ore, Dunlop) at the height of their cohesion after four years together, revisiting classic Monk compositions with unmatched authority. Consensus across Penguin Guide, DownBeat, and Rolling Stone; AllMusic calls it 'some of the finest work Monk ever did in the studio.' This is the album behind Monk's 1964 Time Magazine cover — a defining statement of his mid-1960s quartet period.",
    "case_against": "All eight tracks are re-recordings of compositions Monk had previously released on Blue Note and Prestige — no new material. The 1963 Monk Columbia period is already represented by Monk's Dream (in the canon), and the quartet lineup is the same, so Criss-Cross adds period density rather than new arc or personnel. But artist concentration is not a case-against factor per the rubric.",
    "disagreement": null,
    "recommendation": "Include — a consensus core album that documents Monk's finest working quartet on classic material.",
    "confidence": "high"
  }
